00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:07.339
so how does the loop actually work?

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:11.888
So a normal transformer runs every layer
once from the input right through to

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:17.639
the output What Dory does is it splits
its layers into three separate blocks.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.915
There's an input block at the start
and an output block at the end,

00:03:21.215 --> 00:03:23.224
and each of those only runs once.

00:03:23.506 --> 00:03:28.052
The middle bit is where we've got the
recurrent block, and that's the part that

00:03:28.064 --> 00:03:31.439
loops if we sort of look at how those
layers are actually spread out, you've

00:03:31.445 --> 00:03:35.583
got 16 for the input block, you've got
16 for the output, and then you've got

00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:39.452
the recurrent block, which is actually
40 layers so if you look at the animation

00:03:39.455 --> 00:03:43.718
here, what you're seeing here is a
token going round that recurrent block.

00:03:43.951 --> 00:03:48.396
And we can set it, to be,
once round, twice round, four

00:03:48.396 --> 00:03:50.174
times round, eight times round

00:03:50.474 --> 00:03:54.325
And you can think of it, as those
representations as it goes through

00:03:54.562 --> 00:03:59.659
the recurrent part are just
refining those representations to

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:04.071
get better and better Now, so it
doesn't go totally off on its loops.

00:04:04.263 --> 00:04:08.515
You can see that it's actually
sort of re-adding what came out of

00:04:08.516 --> 00:04:11.023
the input block to it each time.

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:15.150
so in many ways it's very much like
the ResNet models, where you've got

00:04:15.183 --> 00:04:20.826
the original input, being added to a
modification of it The difference here

00:04:20.842 --> 00:04:24.891
is just that that modification, rather
than being different sets of weights

00:04:24.922 --> 00:04:29.511
each time for each block, it's basically
the same set of weights that are just

00:04:29.531 --> 00:04:33.325
getting reinforced here so you can kind
of think of this as every lap that it

00:04:33.325 --> 00:04:37.864
goes through, the model gets reminded of
what the token is actually working on So

00:04:37.864 --> 00:04:41.488
in some ways it's kind of like rereading
the question or rereading the input,

00:04:41.744 --> 00:04:46.951
basically before you have another shot at
getting the best output Now, if we were

00:04:46.955 --> 00:04:52.170
to unroll it like this, you can see that
we're getting a much deeper model, here.

00:04:52.272 --> 00:04:57.074
even though the weights for that repeated
block are the same each time compute

00:04:57.176 --> 00:05:00.536
wise , we've got a lot more depth going on
there the other thing that's interesting

00:05:00.536 --> 00:05:04.043
here and that makes it perhaps more
practical is that the output block is

00:05:04.044 --> 00:05:10.100
trained to read the state after any of
the loops . So one loop, two loops, four

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:12.713
loops are all valid models for this.

00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:17.506
You don't have to retrain it for
any particular number of loops.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.789
You can basically just set that at
inference time and you're making use of

00:05:20.826 --> 00:05:23.223
eight times through these shared weights

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:28.434
So this whole idea is what Nvidia
calls this elastic inference compute.

00:05:28.643 --> 00:05:33.503
And as I turn up these loops here,
the compute per token is going up.

00:05:33.703 --> 00:05:36.126
but the weights in memory
don't change at all.

00:05:36.426 --> 00:05:40.791
In this case, it's still only 3.9
billion parameters sitting on the GPU.

00:05:41.087 --> 00:05:46.616
So already you should start to see that
one of the huge advantages of this is

00:05:46.618 --> 00:05:51.501
that if we can just loop through the same
weights multiple times, we don't need to

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:56.655
have GPUs with huge amounts of memory to
be able to run a model like this You can

00:05:56.656 --> 00:06:01.665
see here at one loop, we've basically
got 72 layers of effective depth.

00:06:01.964 --> 00:06:06.241
At two loops, we're up to 112
If we jump to four loops, we're

00:06:06.254 --> 00:06:08.808
already at 192 layers there.

00:06:09.069 --> 00:06:14.554
But still, it's the same amount of GPU
RAM being used for this and you can see

00:06:14.575 --> 00:06:18.992
looking at the for loop version here,
Dory is actually doing roughly the compute

00:06:19.054 --> 00:06:23.909
of a 9B dense model, even though the
memory footprint of the weights in here

00:06:23.971 --> 00:06:28.880
is the size of a 4B model now it's not
a total win for your memory, et cetera.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.638
Obviously one place where the
memory will grow is the KV cache.

00:06:32.928 --> 00:06:36.308
in per loop mode, every loop
actually keeps its own cache.

00:06:36.465 --> 00:06:41.735
So more loops here means that you will
need a bigger KV cache and memory to

00:06:41.736 --> 00:06:46.023
actually support that in here now you can
actually run it in a sort of last loop

00:06:46.048 --> 00:06:50.509
mode where at each recurrent attention
layer keeps just one cache and then

00:06:50.545 --> 00:06:56.035
overwrites the old ones and you can see if
we go from one loop to four loops there,

00:06:56.203 --> 00:06:58.809
that last loop, doesn't change at all.

00:06:58.869 --> 00:07:02.772
so that is something that you can
actually try out to see how it goes

00:07:03.072 --> 00:07:07.856
Now NVIDIA says that the accuracy and
output length stay close to per loop.

00:07:08.156 --> 00:07:13.053
So give you a sense of it, at a 32 context
window and two loops, you're looking

00:07:13.053 --> 00:07:18.676
at roughly two gig per sequence, versus
just one loop or the last loop out,

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:21.620
and then it's only gonna require, 1.32

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.124
So you could imagine that this is
where you set it up so that if you're

00:07:25.125 --> 00:07:29.309
serving long context with lots of
concurrent users, then this is a

00:07:29.316 --> 00:07:34.115
setting you certainly would look at
trying out to just use the last loop as

00:07:34.115 --> 00:07:36.545
opposed to the per loop KV cache Okay.

